One of the biggest annoyances with using the fantastic host_callback.id_tap as a logging solution is its behaviour in a jax.remat (making a call to id_tap for every remat). When you have a codebase with multiple layers of nested remats, you get spammed with calls to host_callback.id_tap, which both increases the chances of a slowdown due to Python callback overhead, but also makes it hard to efficiently get rid of spurious duplicated logs coming from remats.
Is there any way of implementing an option to id_tap to avoid calling it multiple times in a remat, or some other workaround?
@mattjj , @gnecula

@jax.jit
def f():
  host_callback.id_tap(print, 2.3)
  host_callback.barrier_wait()
  return 3.4

f()
# hangs
Seems bad! The user who found this suggested that perhaps host_callback.barrier_wait() should be an error when staging out code with jit. That probably means it should also be an error for pmap. What about control flow staging?
@gnecula any thoughts?
also cc @LenaMartens @dougalm

I've been playing around with sparse arrays based on WIP #422 and I'm struggling to wrap my head around how to implement custom magic methods, e.g. __matmul__. It seems tracers redirect __matmul__ to AbstractValue._matmul the only implementation I can find in the code is a rather simple _eq.
What I've tried.
I've added COO and AbstractCOO classes from the #422 with the below:
class COO(SparseArray):
  ...
  def __matmul__(self, v):
    return self.matvec(v)

clss AbstractCOO(core.ShapedArray):
  ...
  def _matmul(self, traced, v):
    return coo_matvec_p.bind(traced, v)
A basic test shows concrete evaluation seems to be working fine, but abstract evaluation fails.
def matmul(a, x):
    return a @ x

a = COO(
    jnp.array([[0, 1], [0, 1]], dtype=jnp.int32), jnp.array([4, 3], dtype=jnp.float32)
)
x = jnp.arange(2, dtype=jnp.float32)
print(matmul(a, x))  # works
print(jax.jit(matmul)(a, x))  # TypeError
TypeError: _coo_matvec_impl() takes 2 positional arguments but 3 were given
Further investigation shows the implementation is being passed (data, coords, v).
I've played around with coo_matvec_p.abstract_eval too, but I'm really clutching at straws...

